KSSB 1 requires disclosures of material sustainability-related risks and opportunities, aligning with the TCFD’s pillars: Governance, Strategy, Risk Management, and Metrics/Targets. Companies must correct errors, disclose uncertainties, and include past-period metrics. Industry-specific metrics are optional. KSSB 2 focuses on climate-related risks, requiring disclosure of Scope 1 and 2 emissions, and optionally Scope 3, reflecting South Korea''s phased approach to readiness. KSSB 101 allows voluntary disclosures tied to local policy goals, such as addressing demographic challenges.

Despite significant progress in emissions reporting, gaps remain in TCFD alignment, particularly in Governance and Targets/ Metrics. Companies will face challenges meeting IFRS S2-aligned requirements due to increased granularity.', 'The Korea Sustainability Standards Board (KSSB) introduced the Korean Sustainability Disclosure Standards (KSDS) on May 2, 2024, with mandatory climate-related disclosures to begin in 2026. The standards aim to enhance transparency and align with global disclosure frameworks, particularly IFRS Sustainability Disclosure Standards and TCFD recommendations. The KSDS framework consists of three parts: KSSB 1 (general requirements), KSSB 2 (climate-related disclosures), and KSSB 101 (voluntary, country-specific disclosures).

