The Code outlines seven key principles, including the development of a clear stewardship policy, monitoring investee companies, engaging with management, and exercising voting rights responsibly. It encourages public disclosure of stewardship activities and emphasizes alignment with the interests of beneficiaries and clients. Institutional investors are also required to avoid conflicts of interest and disclose how these are managed.

While voluntary, the Code serves as a benchmark for responsible investment and governance practices in South Korea, influencing institutional investors such as asset managers, pension funds, and insurers.', 'The Korea Stewardship Code, introduced in 2016, establishes principles to guide institutional investors in fulfilling their fiduciary responsibilities and promoting long-term value creation. The Code aligns with global stewardship practices and emphasizes transparency, accountability, and engagement with investee companies. It seeks to improve governance, ensure responsible investment practices, and enhance corporate sustainability in South Korea.

